/* v4 learner-facing rewrites: Themes (7/7 including t.what in base file) */
Object.assign(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY||(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY={}),{
't.cov':{title:'Covenant: how promise becomes a relationship with obligations',dek:'Covenant language connects promise, belonging, responsibility, failure, and renewal across the biblical story.',body:['Covenant is more than a contract in the modern commercial sense. In biblical texts it describes a committed relationship established through promises and obligations. God’s promise to Abraham centers on descendants, land, and blessing. Sinai gives Israel a communal covenant shaped by worship and law. Davidic covenant language focuses on dynasty and kingship.','Prophets later speak from inside covenant failure. Jeremiah can announce judgment and still imagine a “new covenant” in which instruction is internalized. Christian writers then interpret Jesus through covenant language, especially around the Last Supper, forgiveness, and a renewed people.','Tracing covenant well means preserving those different settings. Abraham’s promise, Sinai’s national covenant, Davidic kingship, Jeremiah’s exilic hope, and New Testament interpretations belong in conversation without becoming interchangeable.'],plain:'Covenant is the Bible’s language of committed relationship: promise creates belonging, and belonging carries responsibility.',visual:{type:'theme-thread',title:'Covenant through the canon',stops:[{book:'Genesis 12–17',title:'Promise to Abraham',text:'Family, land, blessing outward'},{book:'Exodus 19–24',title:'Sinai',text:'A rescued people receives covenant responsibilities'},{book:'2 Samuel 7',title:'Davidic promise',text:'Kingship and dynasty hope'},{book:'Jeremiah 31',title:'New-covenant hope',text:'Renewal after failure'},{book:'Gospels / Hebrews',title:'Christian rereading',text:'Jesus interpreted through covenant language'}]},challenge:{kind:'compare-board',title:'Trace covenant without collapsing it',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Good tracing','Needs qualification','Flattening'],items:['Name the audience and promise in each covenant text.','Recognize continuity while preserving different covenant settings.','Treat Abraham, Sinai, David, and Jeremiah as the exact same covenant scene.','Assume later Christian interpretation erases Israel’s earlier covenant setting.'],answer:[0,0,2,2],hints:['Connection is not identity.'],why:'Covenant develops across distinct relationships and historical moments.'}},
't.exile':{title:'Exile and return: from historical disaster to a larger biblical metaphor',dek:'Exile begins as concrete displacement and loss, then becomes language for estrangement, longing, and restoration.',body:['The Assyrian and Babylonian conquests create real political and human displacement. Jerusalem’s destruction in 586 BC becomes the defining exile crisis for Judah: temple, monarchy, land security, and communal life are shattered.','Psalms and prophets use exile to voice grief and hope. Ezekiel imagines restored presence and renewed life. Return under Persia is real but partial, which keeps restoration language alive rather than closing the theme.','Later Jewish and Christian writers can use exile-like language more broadly for alienation and homecoming. That metaphorical development should not erase the historical people whose displacement gave the image its force.'],plain:'Exile is first a real historical loss. Its power as a spiritual metaphor comes from that concrete history, not instead of it.',visual:{type:'theme-thread',title:'Exile → return → larger restoration',stops:[{book:'2 Kings / Lamentations',title:'Jerusalem falls',text:'Political and communal catastrophe'},{book:'Ezekiel',title:'Hope in displacement',text:'Presence and restoration imagined'},{book:'Ezra–Nehemiah',title:'Return',text:'Rebuilding under Persian rule'},{book:'Later biblical use',title:'Homecoming language expands',text:'Estrangement/restoration become larger theological images'}]},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Keep metaphor attached to history',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Biblical exile includes forced displacement and political collapse.','Return is partial and leaves some hopes unresolved.','“Exile” is only a private feeling with no historical referent.','Spiritual uses of exile make the historical deportations irrelevant.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Metaphor gains force from the history.'],why:'The exile theme expands without ceasing to be rooted in concrete historical trauma.'}},
't.sac':{title:'Sacrifice and atonement: more than one biblical image for repaired relationship',dek:'Sacrifice language develops from Israel’s worship into multiple New Testament explanations of Jesus’ saving work.',body:['Levitical sacrifices do not all perform one identical function. Offerings can express thanksgiving, fellowship, dedication, purification, restitution, or atonement. The Day of Atonement in Leviticus 16 is a particularly important ritual context for cleansing and removal of impurity/sin.','New Testament writers use sacrificial language for Jesus, but they also use reconciliation, ransom, victory, representation, covenant, and liberation. Hebrews compares Christ with priesthood and sacrifice; Paul uses reconciliation and participation; other texts emphasize Passover or victory over powers.','Atonement theology becomes distorted when one metaphor is made to cancel every other one. Penal substitution is an important Christian account, but responsible teaching can explain it alongside other biblical images and note where traditions emphasize them differently.'],plain:'The Bible has a family of images for how God deals with sin and restores relationship. Sacrifice is central, but it is not the only image.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Atonement images in conversation',columns:[{title:'Sacrificial / representative language',items:['Offering','Priesthood','Bearing sin','Covenant blood']},{title:'Relational / conflict language',items:['Reconciliation','Liberation','Ransom','Victory over sin/death']}],shared:['Christ’s death and resurrection are central to Christian salvation']},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match image to emphasis',prompt:'Connect each image with the concern it highlights.',items:['Sacrifice','Reconciliation','Victory','Ransom/liberation'],options:['Sin dealt with through offering/representation','Relationship restored','Defeat of hostile powers including sin/death','Release from bondage'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['Do not force every image into “payment.”'],why:'The New Testament explains Christ’s saving work through multiple overlapping images.'}},
't.mercy':{title:'Mercy and steadfast love: compassion that refuses to stay abstract',dek:'Biblical mercy is repeatedly shown in forgiveness, rescue, patience, welcome, and concrete treatment of vulnerable people.',body:['Hebrew and Greek vocabulary translated as mercy, compassion, kindness, or steadfast love carries overlapping but not identical meanings. The theme is larger than one word search. It appears whenever covenant loyalty, forgiveness, rescue, care, or patient compassion shapes the story.','Jonah is angry that Nineveh receives mercy. Hosea and the Psalms speak of steadfast love amid failure. Jesus’ parables portray joy over restoration and neighbor-love that crosses social boundaries. The Gospels also repeatedly connect compassion with feeding, healing, and attention to people in distress.','Mercy is not pretending harm is harmless. Biblical mercy can coexist with truth, justice, boundaries, repentance, and repair. The theme becomes sentimental only when compassion is separated from what people actually need.'],plain:'Mercy is compassion that moves toward people in need or failure without pretending that harm does not matter.',visual:{type:'theme-thread',title:'Mercy across different settings',stops:[{book:'Psalms',title:'Steadfast love',text:'God’s loyal compassion is praised and requested'},{book:'Jonah',title:'Mercy resisted',text:'The prophet resents compassion for enemies'},{book:'Gospels',title:'Compassion embodied',text:'Welcome, healing, feeding, forgiveness'},{book:'Letters',title:'Mercy becomes community practice',text:'Forgiveness, generosity, patient care'}]},challenge:{kind:'scenario',title:'Mercy without denial',prompt:'Choose responses that preserve both compassion and truth.',stages:[{prompt:'Someone has caused real harm and asks for mercy. What is the better starting point?',choices:['Take the harm seriously while seeking a response that is not driven by vengeance.','Call the harm unimportant so mercy is easier.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: mercy need not deny the victim’s reality.','Reconsider: minimizing harm is not compassion.']},{prompt:'A vulnerable person needs practical help. What does mercy look like?',choices:['Move from sympathy toward concrete care where possible.','Offer only a religious slogan.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: biblical compassion repeatedly becomes action.','Reconsider: mercy is more than sentiment.']}],hints:['Compassion and accountability are not opposites.'],why:'Biblical mercy is active compassion that can coexist with truth, justice, and repair.'}},
't.faith':{title:'Faithfulness under pressure is not a guarantee of immediate success',dek:'Daniel, the Psalms, prophets, Jesus, and Hebrews repeatedly distinguish faithfulness from a formula that forces good outcomes.',body:['Biblical stories sometimes end in dramatic rescue, but not always. Daniel 3 presents faithful refusal before the outcome is known: the three Judeans say God can deliver them, but even if not, they will not worship the image. That “even if not” matters.','Hebrews 11 celebrates both people who escape danger and people who suffer or die. The chapter does not use faith as a mechanism that guarantees the preferred immediate result. Faithfulness can mean endurance, courage, trust, and obedience when circumstances remain unresolved.','This protects pastoral care from a cruel distortion: blaming people for illness, persecution, poverty, or unanswered prayer because their faith was supposedly insufficient. Biblical faith can seek healing and rescue while still making room for lament and uncertainty.'],plain:'Faithfulness means trusting and acting faithfully under pressure; it does not give a person control over every outcome.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Faithful people can face different outcomes',columns:[{title:'Rescue',items:['Deliverance from danger','Answered petition','Restored circumstances']},{title:'Endurance without immediate rescue',items:['Suffering continues','Loss occurs','Faithfulness remains meaningful']}],shared:['Outcome is not a simple measurement of faith']},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Faith or success formula?',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Daniel 3 expresses faithfulness before the outcome is known.','Hebrews 11 includes faithful people who suffer.','Suffering proves a person lacked faith.','Enough faith guarantees every requested result.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Read the “even if not” logic.'],why:'Biblical faithfulness cannot be reduced to guaranteed short-term success.'}},
't.all':{title:'Fourteen threads, one rule: connection must never erase context',dek:'Whole-canon theme mastery means tracing multiple recurring concerns while preserving differences in genre, audience, and historical setting.',body:['The Canonical Shelf tracks fourteen recurring threads: covenant; exile and return; kingdom and king; sacrifice and atonement; wilderness and testing; remnant; temple and presence; judgment; mercy and steadfast love; redemption and rescue; wisdom and fear of God; faithfulness under pressure; worship and lament; mission outward.','These themes overlap. Exodus can be about redemption, covenant, wilderness, presence, worship, judgment, mercy, and mission at once. That does not mean every theme is equally central in every passage. A responsible reader asks which thread the text itself foregrounds.','Whole-canon tracing becomes mature when you can explain both continuity and development: how temple/presence moves from tabernacle and temple into later visions; how kingdom language moves from Israel’s monarchy into Jesus’ proclamation; how mission outward can connect Abrahamic blessing, prophetic hope, and Acts without turning each into the same commission.'],plain:'A theme is a route through the library, not a shortcut around each book.',visual:{type:'theme-thread',title:'How to trace any of the fourteen themes',stops:[{book:'First appearance',title:'Name the local meaning',text:'What does the theme do here?'},{book:'Later reuse',title:'Compare',text:'What stays and what changes?'},{book:'Crisis/reframing',title:'Watch development',text:'How does failure or new context reshape it?'},{book:'New Testament use',title:'Identify interpretation',text:'How is earlier language reread?'},{book:'Whole-canon synthesis',title:'State connection with limits',text:'Do not erase the stops'}]},challenge:{kind:'capstone',title:'Choose the mature theme method',prompt:'Which approach best traces a biblical theme?',clues:['The same theme can appear in multiple genres.','Later writers can reuse earlier images in new contexts.','Shared vocabulary does not automatically mean identical meaning.'],options:['Collect matching English words and ignore context.','Explain each passage locally, then compare continuity and development.','Choose one verse as the definition of every occurrence.','Assume later interpretation makes earlier settings irrelevant.'],answer:1,hints:['Local meaning comes before synthesis.'],why:'Whole-canon theme work is comparative interpretation, not proof-text collection.'}}
});
